 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Relates to the minimum requirements for a quorum; there shall be no less than a majority of the total number of members of the commission in order to transact any business, perform any duty or exercise any power.",
 "title": "Relates to the minimum requirements for a quorum",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A6826",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A6826-2017",
     "printNo": "A6826",
     "session": 2017,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 210\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 6826-A, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT to amend the public service law, in relation to the minimum\n      requirements for a quorum\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This bill would amend Public Service  Law  Section  11  to  require  a\nmajority of the total number of members of the Public Service Commission\n(PSC)  to  constitute a quorum, and no less than a majority of the total\nnumber of members be required to vote affirmatively  to  transact  busi-\nness.\n \n  Under  the existing law, the PSC consists of at least five members and\na majority of the commissioners in attendance constitutes a quorum.  The\nproposed  legislation seeks to clarify that there must be at least three\nPSC commissioners present in order to constitute a quorum and  that  all\nthree  commissioners  must  vote affirmatively to take action. While the\nimpetus behind the bill is understandable given the many complex matters\nthe PSC addresses, adhering to its requirements  would  potentially  put\nthe  public at risk. Because the PSC's responsibilities include address-\ning utility system emergencies and ruling on utility rates, the inabili-\nty to act could jeopardize the public welfare, result in utility service\ninterruptions, and rate increases that  -  without  PSC  intervention  -\nwould take effect by operation of law. In those situations, the PSC must\nrely on the commissioners available to participate.\n \n  Moreover,  in  1954,  when the current majority quorum requirement was\nestablished, the materials sent to the  Legislature  and  the  Executive\nrecognized   that  given  the  potential  for  vacancies,  illness,  and\nabsences, that the stricter quorum requirement then in effect would have\na potentially \"crippling effect\" on the PSC. Accordingly, the  Executive\nand  Legislature  agreed  the current construct would provide a suitable\nbalance. I cannot put the public's health, safety, and welfare  at  risk\nby regressing to an antiquated system last in place over 60 years ago.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
    "vetoNumber": 210,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
